*======================================================================
* kdefs.a99 - the kernel's definitions: hardware addresses, the memory
* map (CPU, scratchpad, slot A, SAMS pages, VRAM), key codes, colour
* roles, screen geometry, file-service constants.
*
* Every kernel source, the loader and every module include this file or
* its export, kernel.equ (a module cannot COPY kdefs.a99: the kernel is
* assembled first and its symbols, these included, are written to
* build/kernel.equ).  The application's own numbers (its name, how many
* SAMS pages it needs, the page of each of its modules) come from
* appcfg.a99, which tools/skbuild.py writes from app.json.
*
* SAMS Kernel 1.0.  MIT License, (c) 2026 Rebecca Sutter.
*======================================================================
* HOW TO READ THIS FILE.  kdefs.a99 is the kernel's vocabulary and its memory
* map in one place; every name a program uses that is not a routine is here.
* Read it in the order it is written: hardware ports; the SAMS page numbers
* the kernel fixes; the scratchpad (the fast 256 bytes at >8300, where the
* CPU's registers live, so the kernel's own hot variables share it with the
* console's); slot A (>2000-3FFF), which the kernel uses at its start and
* gives the rest of to your application as APPRAM; the video memory map; the
* key codes; the colour roles; the screen geometry; the file-service
* constants.  Each EQU gives a name to a number and reserves nothing: where a
* name stands for a variable, the comment says how big it is, and a program
* must not use an address that is not its own.
*
* The golden rule of the map: your application's RAM is APPRAM (>2300-3EFF,
* shared by all your modules) and MODRAM (>2200, per module); everything else
* in slot A (>2000-21FF) and the scratchpad belongs to the kernel, and a
* program that writes there corrupts the keyboard, the file service or the
* page pool in ways that are very hard to find.

       COPY "appcfg.a99"
       COPY "macros.a99"

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Hardware
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* VDP ports
VDPRD  EQU  >8800            VRAM read data
VDPSTA EQU  >8802            VDP status register
VDPWD  EQU  >8C00            VRAM write data
VDPWA  EQU  >8C02            VRAM / VDP register address

* GROM ports (the loader lifts the console font for its refusal screens)
GRMRD  EQU  >9800            GROM read data (auto-increments)
GRMWA  EQU  >9C02            GROM address, high byte first

* Sound
SOUND  EQU  >8400            TMS9919 sound chip

* AMS / SAMS mapper
* CRU base: bit 0 = registers visible, bit 1 = mapping on
AMSCRU EQU   >1E00
* mapper registers, one word per 4K window: >4000 + 2*window
AMSREG EQU   >4000

* Cartridge bank select (write any value to >6000 + 2*bank)
CBANK0 EQU  >6000            cart bank 0 select; bank n at >6000 + 2n

* The console's interrupt routine (>0900, workspace >83C0) runs only inside
* VSYNC, where interrupts are briefly allowed.  It advances the frame
* counter, plays sound lists, and blanks the screen when its timer wraps
* (about nine minutes at 60 Hz).  The kernel clears that timer on every key,
* so the screen blanks after nine idle minutes, as the TI's own programs do.
ISRTMR EQU  >8379            frame counter kept by the console ISR
ISRFLG EQU  >83C2            >50 = no sprite motion, no QUIT key
SNDPTC EQU  >83CC            sound list pointer
SNDDUR EQU  >83CE            sound list duration counter (0 = finished)
* the console ISR adds this byte (R14's high byte in the GPL workspace) to
* >8379
TMRINC EQU   >83FC
* every frame: VSYNC needs it non-zero, and a DSR may disturb it
SNDFLG EQU  >83FD            bit 0 set = the sound list is in VDP RAM
* console's copy of VDP register 1 (written back when it blanks)
VR1CPY EQU   >83D4
* console screen-blank timer (+2 a frame; blanks at 0)
SCRTMO EQU   >83D6
GPLWS  EQU  >83E0            the GPL workspace: DSRs run in it

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* SAMS pages (4K each; identity-mapped at boot, so page n of the fixed
* windows sits at n<<12).  The kernel owns pages >00-1F's fixed roles;
* from PGDATA up the build assigns the application's pages (appcfg.a99).
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
PGLOWA EQU  >02              slot A (>2000, >3000): state and the stack
PGKERN EQU  >0A              resident kernel (>A000, >B000)
* slot C's default pair (>C000, >D000): modules run here
PGSLTC EQU   >0C
* slot B's default pair (>E000, >F000): data is paged here
PGLOWB EQU   >0E
PGDATA EQU  >10              data bank: glyphs, colour schemes (2 pages)
PGMOD0 EQU  >12              the first page the build gives a module
* Pages >04-09 are free for the application (the CPU's windows >4000-9FFF are
* not RAM, so these pages exist only when mapped into slot B or C).  The
* loader's memory test starts at PGDATA, so they are not tested at boot.
* Pages >00-01 are the console's windows and are never used.

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Scratchpad (>8300 - >83FF).  R0-R15 live in the workspace, so the
* console's own variables sit among ours.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
WS     EQU  >8300            the one workspace: R0-R15 (LWPI WS)
HOT    EQU  >8340            kernel hot variables
* 16-bit frame counter (VSYNC and the idle polls add 1)
FRAME  EQU   HOT+0
* The console's floating-point routines (FADD, FMUL, the conversions ...) keep
* their working registers in the scratchpad: FAC at >834A-8351 and ARG at
* >835C-8363, and they change bytes up to >836F (measured: >834A-8353,
* >8356-8359, >835C-8361, >8365-8369).  Nothing of the kernel's is in >834A-836F
* (since 1.21.0: until then F18VER, NPAGES, QUITF and VDPPIC were, and a float
* call corrupted them).  HOT+10 to HOT+15 and >8350-836F: never put a
* variable there.  The package also uses >8375 (the sign of FAC), >8376 (a copy
* of its exponent), >8377 and the GPL status byte >837C: nor there.
* lib/float.a99 calls the routines.
* F18A firmware version (status register 14), e.g. >0019
F18VER EQU   HOT+2
* SAMS pages present: 32, 64, 128, 256 ... 4096 (a 16M card)
NPAGES EQU   HOT+4
* non-zero: the application asks to quit to the TI title
QUITF  EQU   HOT+6
* >0001 if an F18A answered (the loader refuses otherwise)
F18FLG EQU   HOT+8
* non-zero: the F18A is a PICO9918 (in slot A: the loader's detection runs
* after it has cleared slot A; it is read rarely)
VDPPIC EQU   >20AA
* 64 bytes: the loader's relocated bank copier (free afterwards)
SCRTCH EQU   >8380

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Slot A (>2000 - >3FFF, pages >02-03): the kernel's state first, then
* the application's.  Nothing remaps slot A.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
KEYMAP EQU  >2000            6 bytes: keyboard columns 0-5 (0 bit = pressed)
* last chord code produced (word, code in the high byte), for auto-repeat
KLAST  EQU   >2006
KHOLD  EQU  >2008            frames the current chord has been held
* modifiers of the last code: >80 FCTN, >40 SHIFT, >20 CTRL, >10 ALPHA
KMODS  EQU   >200A
ALOCK  EQU  >200C            >0001 while ALPHA LOCK is down
KHEAD  EQU  >200E            key buffer: next slot to write (0-63)
KTAIL  EQU  >2010            key buffer: next slot to read (0-63)
KCOUNT EQU  >2012            codes taken from the buffer since boot (for tests)
KCURK  EQU  >2014            the key KDECOD found (column * 8 + row), or -1
KLKEY  EQU  >2016            the key that produced KLAST, or -1
KBUF   EQU  >2040            64-byte key buffer (codes, one byte each)
SCHEME EQU  >2080            current colour scheme (0-4)
TXTBUF EQU  >2090            16 bytes: number formatting and the hex routines
MCENT  EQU  >20A0            MODCALL: the entry
MCRET  EQU  >20A2            MODCALL: R0 from the call
* GOMOD: the module to switch to (first page), 0 = none
NEXTPG EQU   >20A4
GOARG  EQU  >20A6            GOMOD: the argument handed to its INIT
* the module that was running before the last switch (0 at boot)
PREVPG EQU   >20A8
GPUBUF EQU  >20B0            8 bytes: the GPU mailbox as built (VSHIFT)
* non-zero: the F18A has no DMA engine (T80INI's test)
NODMA  EQU   >20B8
VMNUM  EQU  >20E8            the video mode set by VMODE (0-4)
VMOPT  EQU  >20EA            its options
TXCOLS EQU  >20EC            columns of the text screen (80, 40 or 32)
TXROWS EQU  >20EE            rows (24 or 30)
TXCELLS EQU >20F0            columns x rows
* non-zero: this mode has an attribute table the text routines write
TXATTR EQU   >20F2
* the name table's VRAM address (NAMTAB, or NAMGM2 in Graphics II)
NAMBAS EQU   >20F4
* The page-pool bitmap (a bit per page, 1 = free) is in the kernel's own pages,
* at the top of them.  POOLBM (its address), POOLMX (how many pages it covers:
* 4096 unless app.json says "pool_max_pages") and POOLWD (its size in words)
* are written by the build into appcfg.a99, which this file copies; the build
* refuses a kernel that grows into it.
POOLLO EQU  >20E0            pool: the first page it manages
POOLHI EQU  >20E2            pool: the page after its last
FREECT EQU  >20E4            pool: free pages

* Music player (music.a99): >21C0->21DF
MUSTAT EQU  >21C0            non-zero: a song is playing
MUPAUS EQU  >21C2            non-zero: paused
MUPAGE EQU  >21C4            the song's first SAMS page
MUPOS  EQU  >21C6            the next command's offset in the song (0-65535)
MUWAIT EQU  >21C8            frames still to hold the current sound
MULOOP EQU  >21CA            the loop target's offset (>FFFF: none)
MULAT  EQU  >21CC            the sound register the last latch byte chose (0-7)
* 8 bytes: each register's last latch byte (for resuming)
MUSHL  EQU   >21CE
MUSHD  EQU  >21D6            8 bytes: each frequency register's last data byte

* Sound effects (music.a99, app.json "sfx": true): >20F6->20FF
SFXST  EQU  >20F6            non-zero: an effect is playing
SFXPG  EQU  >20F8            the effect's first SAMS page
SFXPOS EQU  >20FA            the next command's offset in the effect
SFXWT  EQU  >20FC            frames still to hold the current sound
SFXOWN EQU  >20FE            channels the effect owns (bit c = channel c)

* The page each window holds, tracked by the pager (a page is 0-4095):
CURA2  EQU  >21E0            window >2000 (slot A)
CURA3  EQU  >21E2            window >3000
CURBE  EQU  >21E4            window >E000 (slot B)
CURBF  EQU  >21E6            window >F000
* window >C000 (slot C: the running module's first page)
CURCC  EQU   >21E8
CURCD  EQU  >21EA            window >D000

* Joysticks (keys.a99), read every KPOLL: a state is
* JFIRE|JLEFT|JRIGHT|JDOWN|JUP
JFIRE  EQU  1
JLEFT  EQU  2
JRIGHT EQU  4
JDOWN  EQU  8
JUP    EQU  16
JOY1   EQU  >21EC            joystick 1's state now (1 = active)
JOY2   EQU  >21EE            joystick 2's
JOYN1  EQU  >21F0            joystick 1's presses since JOYGET last looked
JOYN2  EQU  >21F2
* non-zero: a joystick types arrow keys and ENTER (1: joystick 1, 2: joystick
* 2, 3: both)
JOYKEY EQU   >21F4
JKLAST EQU  >21F6            the code the joystick is typing now
JKHOLD EQU  >21F8            frames it has held it (for the repeat)

* The clock (CLKGET, files.a99), read from TIPI's PI.CLOCK: the words at >20C0
DTDOW  EQU  >20C0            day of the week, as the clock gives it (a digit)
DTMON  EQU  >20C2            month 1-12
DTDAY  EQU  >20C4            day 1-31
DTYEAR EQU  >20C6            year, two digits (add 2000)
DTHOUR EQU  >20C8            hour 0-23
DTMIN  EQU  >20CA            minute
DTSEC  EQU  >20CC            second (0 if the clock gives none)
* non-zero once a program has used the joysticks (JOYGET): see JOYPOLL
JOYON  EQU   >20CE

* File service (files.a99): >2100->21FF
FHOT   EQU  >2100            96 bytes: scratchpad >8320->837F while a DSR runs
FNAMB  EQU  >2160            8 bytes: the device name for the DSR search
FSR10  EQU  >2168            R10 across the DSR
FDCRU  EQU  >216A            CRU base of the ROM that answered
FDENT  EQU  >216C            what DLGPL calls
FD54   EQU  >216E            >8354, >8356, >83D0, >83D2 across the power-up
FD56   EQU  >2170
FDD0   EQU  >2172
FDD2   EQU  >2174
FDPWR  EQU  >2176            non-zero once the controller's power-up has run
FD70   EQU  >2178            >8370 as the controller's power-up left it
FSELH  EQU  >21FA            the file handle the F routines act on (0-2)
FPFLG  EQU  >21FC            scratch: a PAB flag byte's address
FDPW2  EQU  >21FE            scratch: the power-up list entry being called
FERR   EQU  >217A            the last file operation's result
FLEN   EQU  >217C            the last record's length
* the name in use (address of a length-prefixed string)
FNMP   EQU   >217E
FPAB   EQU  >2180            64 bytes: the PAB as built
*                            >21EC->21F9: the joystick variables (below)
*                            >21FA->21FF: the file service's handle (FSELH)

* 256 bytes of scratch for the running module (not kept across a module switch)
MODRAM EQU   >2200
* (>2300 up to APPTOP; app.json "appram_code" puts ARSIZE bytes of the
* program's own code at its start, copied there at start-up, and the data
* begins after it)
APPRAM EQU  >2300+ARSIZE       the application's RAM
APPTOP EQU  >3F00            ... and the software stack above it
* R10 starts here: the first push writes >3FFE (256 bytes: 128 words)
STKTOP EQU   >4000

* The data bank (pages PGDATA, mapped at >E000 by LDSCHM and T80INI)
DGLYPH EQU  >E000            256 glyphs x 8 bytes
DSCHEM EQU  >E800            5 colour schemes x 16 palette words (>0RGB)

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Key codes (a code is a byte; KEYGET returns it in the high byte)
*   >20->7E  printable characters
*   >0D      ENTER (with any modifier but CTRL)
*   >80->BF  CTRL + key: >80 | (key's unshifted code AND >3F)
*            (CTRL A = >81 ... CTRL Z = >9A, CTRL 0 = >B0 ... CTRL 9 = >B9)
*   >C0->FF  FCTN + key with no character: >C0 | (unshifted code AND >3F)
*            (FCTN E = >C5, FCTN S = >D3, FCTN D = >C4, FCTN X = >D8,
*             FCTN 1 = >F1 ... FCTN 9 = >F9, FCTN 0 = >F0, FCTN = = >FD)
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
KUP    EQU  >C5              FCTN E (up arrow)
KLEFT  EQU  >D3              FCTN S (left arrow)
KRIGHT EQU  >C4              FCTN D (right arrow)
KDOWN  EQU  >D8              FCTN X (down arrow)
KDEL   EQU  >F1              FCTN 1 (DEL)
KINS   EQU  >F2              FCTN 2 (INS)
KERASE EQU  >F3              FCTN 3 (ERASE)
KCLEAR EQU  >F4              FCTN 4 (CLEAR)
KBEGIN EQU  >F5              FCTN 5 (BEGIN)
KPROC  EQU  >F6              FCTN 6 (PROC'D)
KAID   EQU  >F7              FCTN 7 (AID)
KREDO  EQU  >F8              FCTN 8 (REDO)
KBACK  EQU  >F9              FCTN 9 (BACK)
KQUIT  EQU  >FD              FCTN = (QUIT)
KENTER EQU  >0D              ENTER

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Colour roles: palette entries 1-15.  A position-attribute byte is
* (foreground role << 4) | background role; a colour scheme is a palette
* of 16 words, so changing scheme recolours the whole screen without
* rewriting a cell.  Entry 0 is transparent (the backdrop shows).
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
RBG    EQU  1                page background
RTEXT  EQU  2                text
RBOLD  EQU  3                emphasis (bold)
RITAL  EQU  4                italic
RBOLIT EQU  5                bold italic
RSCRPT EQU  6                superscript and subscript
RDIM   EQU  7                dim: strikethrough, secondary text
RERROR EQU  8                error
RSELBG EQU  9                selection background
RSELFG EQU  10               selection foreground
RBARBG EQU  11               status bar background
RBARFG EQU  12               status bar foreground
RRULER EQU  13               ruler, rules, frames
RMARK  EQU  14               markers, labels
RACCNT EQU  15               accent: the cursor

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Video modes (video.a99: VMODE) and the VRAM map they share
*
* VMODE R0 = the base mode, R1 = options (added together):
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
VMT80  EQU  0                Text 80: 80 columns, 6 x 8 glyphs (the boot mode)
VMT40  EQU  1                Text 40: 40 columns, 6 x 8 glyphs
VMGM1  EQU  2                Graphics I: 32 columns, 8 x 8 tiles, 256 x 192
* Graphics II (bitmap): 256 x 192, colour per 8 pixels
VMGM2  EQU   3
VMMC   EQU  4                Multicolor: 64 x 48 blocks
VO30   EQU  >0001            30 rows instead of 24 (every mode)
* a colour attribute per screen position (Text 80/40; Graphics I with ECM)
VOPOS  EQU   >0002
* enhanced colour mode 1 for tiles: 1 plane, 2 colours of a palette pair
VOTE1  EQU   >0004
VOTE2  EQU  >0008            ... mode 2: 2 planes, 4 colours
* ... mode 3: 3 planes, 8 colours (Text 80/40 and Graphics I only)
VOTE3  EQU   >000C
VOSE1  EQU  >0010            enhanced colour mode 1 for sprites
VOSE2  EQU  >0020            ... mode 2
VOSE3  EQU  >0030            ... mode 3
VOSPR  EQU  >0040            sprites on (VR51 = 32); off leaves them stopped
VOS16  EQU  >0080            sprites 16 x 16 (else 8 x 8)
VOSMG  EQU  >0100            sprites magnified x2
* the boot mode: Text 80, 30 rows, attribute per position
VMDEF  EQU   VO30+VOPOS

* The text routines' geometry: set by VMODE
* (the boot mode's columns: use TXCOLS in code that must follow the mode)
COLS   EQU   80
ROWS   EQU  30

* VRAM.  Every mode but Graphics II uses this one map, laid out so that the
* enhanced colour modes (which add pattern planes at +>800 and sprite planes at
* +>200) fit beside the sprites and the file service.
NAMTAB EQU  >0000            name table (Graphics II: NAMGM2)
GPUMB  EQU  >0960            the GPU's mailbox (8 bytes: a DMA's settings)
GPUAD  EQU  >0970            the GPU's program (about 26 bytes)
* Graphics I's colour table (32 bytes; with ECM or VOPOS ATRTAB is used)
GM1CT  EQU   >09C0
ATRTAB EQU  >0A00            attribute table (2,400 bytes at 80 x 30)
* sprite attribute table (32 sprites x 4 bytes; links at +>80)
SATAB  EQU   >1400
* pattern table, plane 0 (2,048 bytes); plane 1 at +>800, plane 2 at +>1000
PATTAB EQU   >1800
* sprite patterns, plane 0 (+>200 per plane: 64 patterns of 8 x 8)
SPTAB  EQU   >3000
* Graphics II (the standard bitmap map)
GM2PT  EQU  >0000            patterns (6,144 bytes)
NAMGM2 EQU  >1800            names (768 or 960 bytes)
GM2SAT EQU  >1C00            sprite attribute table
GM2CT  EQU  >2000            colours (6,144 bytes)
* sprite patterns (these overlap the disk controller's area >37D8-3FFF)
GM2SPT EQU   >3800
* free for the application (Text 80, 30 rows): >09E0-09FF, >1500-17FF, and,
* when no
* sprites / ECM planes are used, >2000-35FF in pieces: see docs/VIDEO-MODES.md
* >3600-36FF the file record buffer, >3700-37D7 the PAB, >37D8-3FFF the
* disk controller's own (nothing else may touch it)

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* F18A registers, for VREG (the number written to a register is the
* byte in the high half of R1)
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
VR_MODE0 EQU  0              M3 (bit 1: bitmap), M4 (bit 2: with text, Text 80)
* 16K, display, interrupts, M1 (text), M2 (multicolor), sprite size, magnify
VR_MODE1 EQU   1
VR_NAME  EQU  2              name table, 1K units
VR_COLOR EQU  3              colour table, 64-byte units
VR_PATT  EQU  4              pattern table, 2K units
VR_SAT   EQU  5              sprite attribute table, 128-byte units
VR_SPT   EQU  6              sprite pattern table, 2K units
VR_BACK  EQU  7              text colours (fg/bg) and the backdrop
VR_NAME2 EQU  10             tile layer 2 name table, 1K units
VR_COLR2 EQU  11             tile layer 2 colour table, 64-byte units
VR_STAT  EQU  15             status register select
VR_HINT  EQU  19             horizontal interrupt line (0 = off)
* palette select: sprites bits 5-4, tile layer 2 bits 3-2, tile layer 1 bits
* 1-0
VR_PALSL EQU   24
VR_HS2   EQU  25             tile layer 2 horizontal scroll
VR_VS2   EQU  26             tile layer 2 vertical scroll
VR_HS1   EQU  27             tile layer 1 horizontal scroll
VR_VS1   EQU  28             tile layer 1 vertical scroll
* page sizes of the two layers; plane offsets of tiles (bits 3-2) and sprites
* (7-6)
VR_PAGE EQU   29
VR_SPRMX EQU  30             sprites per scan line (0 = the jumper's choice)
VR_BMPCT EQU  31             bitmap layer control
VR_BMPAD EQU  32             bitmap layer base, 64-byte units
VR_BMPX  EQU  33             bitmap layer x
VR_BMPY  EQU  34             bitmap layer y
VR_BMPW  EQU  35             bitmap layer width (0 = 256)
VR_BMPH  EQU  36             bitmap layer height
VR_PALCT EQU  47             palette data port control
VR_INCR  EQU  48             VRAM address increment (signed)
* TL2 on (bit 7), 30 rows (6), tile ECM (5-4), real Y (3), linking (2), sprite
* ECM (1-0)
VR_ECM EQU   49
* GPU triggers, tile layer 1 off (4), report max sprites (3), scan lines (2),
* position attributes (1), TL2 priority (0)
VR_FLAGS EQU   50
* stop sprite: sprites at this index and above are not drawn (32 = all drawn)
VR_STOP EQU   51
VR_GPUHI EQU  54             GPU start address, high byte
VR_GPULO EQU  55             ... low byte (writing it starts the GPU)
VR_GPUCT EQU  56             GPU control
VR_LOCK  EQU  57             unlock sequence

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* File service (files.a99)
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
FPABV  EQU  >3700            handle 0's PAB in VRAM (handle n: +64 * n)
* the record buffer in VRAM (up to 255 bytes), shared by the handles
FBUFV  EQU   >3600
* files that can be open at once (the controller's default is 3 buffers)
FMAXH  EQU   3
* open flags (the PAB's flag byte, high byte of R0): add one from each group
FDISP  EQU  >0000            DISPLAY records
FINTL  EQU  >0800            INTERNAL records
FFIXED EQU  >0000            FIXED-length records
FVAR   EQU  >1000            VARIABLE-length records
* file type: RELATIVE (records addressed by number: FRREAD, FRWRITE; FIXED
* only)
FREL   EQU   >0100
FUPDT  EQU  >0000            mode: update
FOUT   EQU  >0200            mode: output (creates the file)
FIN    EQU  >0400            mode: input
FAPP   EQU  >0600            mode: append
* the file service's own scratch words (>20D0-20D8): record buffers' addresses
* and the like
FRBUF  EQU  >20D0            FRREAD, FRWRITE: the caller's buffer
FRLEN  EQU  >20D2            FRWRITE: its length
FEXN   EQU  >20D4            FEXIST: the caller's file name
FCRB   EQU  >20D6            FCATOP, FCATNX: the catalog record buffer
FCEA   EQU  >20D8            FCATNX: the entry to fill
* the frame hook and the break flag (1.21.0)
* FRMHK: 0, or the address of a routine the kernel calls once a frame at every
* place it polls the keyboard (see KFRAME); FRMGD is non-zero while it runs
FRMHK  EQU  >20DA
FRMGD  EQU  >20DC
* BRKF: set (>FFFF) by the keyboard decoder when FCTN 4 (KCLEAR) is pressed,
* whether or not the key buffer had room; the program clears it
BRKF   EQU  >20DE
* one file of a catalog, as FCATNX returns it (CE_SIZE bytes)
* name: a length byte and up to 10 characters (11 bytes)
CE_NAME EQU   0
* type: 1 DIS/FIX 2 DIS/VAR 3 INT/FIX 4 INT/VAR 5 program (negative: protected)
CE_TYPE EQU   12
CE_SECT EQU  14              size in sectors
CE_RLEN EQU  16              record length
CE_SIZE EQU  18
* FCATNX: R0 when the catalog has no more files (not a DSR error code)
FCEND  EQU   >00FE
* FSYNC: R0 when the file cannot be reopened at its place (a sequential update
* file)
FNOPL  EQU   >00FD
* results (R0): 0 = done; 1-7 = the PAB's error code; >FF = no such device
FEEOF  EQU  5                reading past the end of the file
* a file error (the stand-in disk's answer for a missing input file;
FENOFI EQU   7
* TI's own controller answers 2 there, and 7 for a close after end of file)
